{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>One portion (shechem) beyond your brothers.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): he says, 'I have already given you in that land the one portion that is in my hand to give — namely, the portion of the firstborn, by which you shall have more than your brothers — for I took it from the hand of the Amorite with my sword and with my bow.' And this portion of the firstborn is what he blessed him with, that his sons should become two tribes in greatness and in the dedication of the princes. And it further includes another portion, namely, the portion that he has in the land; for it is not in his hand to give only one portion alone, for it is impossible for him to seize from one of his sons, but the portion of the firstborn he gave him, which was in his hand to give to whichever of his sons he wished." "<b>And</b> what he said, <b>from the hand of the Amorite</b>, is as if he said 'from the hand of the Canaanite,' but he mentioned the Amorite because he was the mightiest of them all." "<b>And</b> what he said, <b>with my sword and with my bow</b>, is to say that the land of Canaan would not be conquered before them except with sword and bow. And it alludes to what Scripture says (Joshua 11:19): ״לא היתה עיר אשר השלימה את בני ישראל בלתי החוי יושבי גבעון את הכל לקחו במלחמה כי מאת ה' היתה לחזק את לבם לקראת המלחמה את ישראל למען החרימם״. And it should have said 'with a sword and a bow' (בחרב וקשת); the reason he attributed the sword and the bow to himself is that his merit is what fights for them, as it is written (Psalms 44:4): ״כי לא בחרבם ירשו ארץ וזרועם לא הושיעה למו כי ימינך וזרועך ואור פניך כי רציתם״ — that is, by the merit of the patriarchs: כי ימינך, this is Abraham; וזרועך, this is Isaac; ואור פניך, this is Jacob — for the face of Jacob is engraved upon the Chariot. And Onkelos, who translated 'בצלותי ובבעותי,' intended this: for the word בקשתי he derived from the language of בקשה (request, prayer), therefore he translated בעותי; and the reason he translated בחרבי as 'בצלותי' is because prayer protects a person like a sword in the hand of a mighty warrior. Or, בחרבי may mean 'by the merit of the Torah' — for the patriarchs knew it through their intellect and fulfilled it before it was given. And so they expounded regarding Jacob (Genesis 32:5): ״עם לבן גרתי״ — that he observed תרי״ג commandments in his household; and we find that the Torah is likened to a two-edged sword, as they expounded (Psalms 149:6): ״וחרב פיפיות בידם״ — the Torah is like a two-edged sword, which cuts from both sides; and so it is written of the Tablets (Exodus 32:15): ״מזה ומזה הם כתובים״." "<b>Ramban</b>, of blessed memory, writes that it is likely that Jacob did as the prophets would do: he stretched out his hand with a sword toward the land of the Amorite and shot arrows there, so that it would be conquered before him — in the manner that Elisha did (II Kings 13:16–17): ״וישם אלישע ידיו על ידי המלך״, ״ויאמר אלישע ירה ויור״. And regarding this he said: בחרבי ובקשתי. And what he said אשר לקחתי — for from then the land was acquired before him. Thus far [his words]." "<b>There are those</b> who explained 'from the hand of the Amorite' as: the birthright that I took from Esau, who did the deeds of the Amorite; or that he trapped his father with the talk of his mouth — I have given it to you בחרבי ובקשתי, by my merit." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ואני נתתי לך שכם אחד — Shechem itself, literally. He said to him: I am putting you to the trouble of burial, that you will bury me in the Cave of Machpelah, and I have given you a place where you will be buried — as it is written (Joshua 24:32): ״ואת עצמות יוסף אשר העלו בני ישראל ממצרים קברו בשכם״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:27.265145066Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}